          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had reflux for years and now food sometimes sticks going down. How worrying is that, and what does guidance describe about when to investigate?",
          "answer": "Please book a prompt appointment with a doctor. Don't wait for it to get worse. A fixed risk check found no emergency pattern and rated this as routine, but that is not a clearance. The reflux guidance I found, \"GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it\", lists difficulty swallowing as an alarm feature. It says these symptoms call for prompt endoscopy (a camera test of the gullet and stomach) rather than another trial of reflux treatment.\n\nGet same-day or emergency care if you have difficulty breathing, chest pain or pressure, persistent vomiting that stops you keeping fluids down, signs of dehydration, or vomiting blood or black, tarry stools. Severe difficulty swallowing with drooling also needs urgent help. If you cannot swallow your own saliva or fluids, are choking, or feel something is seriously wrong, call emergency services now. My search for a fuller red-flag list failed, so this list may be incomplete.\n\nThe guideline also names other alarm features that should prompt a doctor to act quickly: unintended weight loss, iron-deficiency anaemia, persistent vomiting, a lump you can feel in the upper abdomen or neck, a family history of upper gut cancer, or new symptoms at an older age. Tell your doctor if any of these apply to you.\n\nOn the likely cause, the guidance says long-standing reflux can inflame the gullet (oesophagitis) and can narrow it (stricture), which causes swallowing difficulty. Because a new swallowing problem can have other causes too, guidance favours checking with endoscopy rather than assuming it is reflux. Your doctor can tell you what they think is most likely and what the test is for.\n\nThe material I found did not answer several things. It gave no age threshold, did not compare endoscopy with other tests such as a barium swallow or manometry, and did not say how strong the evidence is for each alarm feature. I found no disagreement between authorities on this, but that does not mean none exists. A clinician can explain which test suits your situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
